WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.470
Learners do not receive new information as a blank slate.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.995
They interpret it through what they already know, what they believe,

00:00:05.995 --> 00:00:08.890
the vocabulary they recognize, and the examples they have seen before.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.940
Prior knowledge can help because it gives new ideas a place to attach.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.575
It can also get in the way when a learner has a misconception, an incomplete model,

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:18.600
or an assumption that works in one context but fails in another.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.890
A mental model is an internal understanding of how something works.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.840
It may be simple, detailed, accurate, incomplete, or wrong in important ways.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.085
Experts often have rich models that let them see patterns quickly.

00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:32.920
Beginners may see the same material as a list of isolated facts because they have not yet

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.400
built the relationships that make the topic coherent.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.515
This is one reason an expert explanation can feel obvious to the expert and confusing to

00:00:39.515 --> 00:00:40.120
the learner.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.440
Better instruction activates prior knowledge before adding complexity.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.160
That can be as simple as asking what learners already do, naming common assumptions,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.840
defining important vocabulary,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.970
or using a familiar analogy before introducing the formal concept.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.790
The goal is not to shame weak models.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.725
The goal is to make them visible, test them against examples,

00:00:56.725 --> 00:01:00.400
and help learners replace them with models that are more useful for the work they actually

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.250
need to perform.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.120
This is also why vocabulary matters.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.030
A learner who misses one key term may lose the thread of an entire explanation.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.640
Good courses define terms when they first matter, revisit them in context,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.540
and show how the terms relate to actions.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.880
The learner is not just collecting definitions.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.760
They are building a map that helps them navigate the subject.
